{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>For all their vows and for all their freewill offerings</b> — \"Their vows\": one who says 'it is upon me.' \"Their freewill offerings\": one who says 'this is it.' This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 22:18). But it appears to me that since Scripture says (Leviticus 22:21), \"to fulfill a vow or as a freewill offering,\" and so it says in every place — in the passage of libations (Numbers 15:3, 8), and likewise \"when one explicitly vows a person's valuation\" (Leviticus 27:2), and likewise in the passage of the Nazirite (Numbers 6:2) — a vow is that which comes concerning a matter that one explicitly requests from Him, that one vows to HaShem in his distress: 'If You act wondrously with me to deliver me from this trouble, I will bring a burnt offering or peace offerings,' in the manner of \"And Jacob vowed a vow, saying, if Elohim will be with me\" (Genesis 28:20), \"And Israel vowed a vow to HaShem and said, 'If You will indeed give'\" (Numbers 21:2), \"and they vowed vows\" (Jonah 1:16), and so all of them. And our Rabbis said (Kinnim 1:1): What is a vow? One who says 'an burnt offering is upon me.' A freewill offering — one who says 'this is a burnt offering.' And what is the difference between a vow and a freewill offering? A vow — if the animal died or was stolen, he is liable for its replacement, etc. They said this to explain the distinction between them, for the way of those who vow in their distress is to say, 'If Elohim will be with me in this matter, it is upon me to bring a burnt offering or a slaughter offering,' and this would not be called a freewill offering in an unqualified sense. But what one sets aside and gives at once — that is a freewill offering, for at the time of giving his spirit moves him voluntarily. Now Scripture says that whether one brings a burnt offering or peace offerings that he vowed upon his distress, or whether he brings them as the freewill offering of his spirit, it must be whole. One might suppose that the vowed offering ought to be required to be whole more than the freewill offering. This is the reason of Scripture:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:58:10.866905752Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}